📝 Conditional Auto‑Configuration Class
src/main/java/com/sgx/thor/autoconfig/ThorAutoConfiguration.java
package com.sgx.thor.autoconfig;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class ThorAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(
        name = "thor.enabled",
        havingValue = "true",
        matchIfMissing = false
    )
    public String thorBanner() {
        return "⚡ Thor Starter Active!";
    }
}
🔎 Explanation
* @ConditionalOnProperty → bean is only created if thor.enabled=true in application.properties.
* If property is missing or set to false, the bean won’t load.

📝 Register Auto‑Configuration
src/main/resources/META-INF/spring.factories
org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.EnableAutoConfiguration=\
com.sgx.thor.autoconfig.ThorAutoConfiguration

📂 Usage in a Project
1. Add Dependency
In your app’s pom.xml:
<dependency>
  <groupId>com.sgx</groupId>
  <artifactId>thor-spring-starter</artifactId>
  <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
</dependency>
2. Configure Property
src/main/resources/application.properties:
thor.enabled=true
3. Use It
@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var ctx = SpringApplication.run(DemoApp.class, args);
        if (ctx.containsBean("thorBanner")) {
            System.out.println(ctx.getBean("thorBanner"));
        } else {
            System.out.println("Thor starter disabled.");
        }
    }
}

✅ Behavior
* With thor.enabled=true → prints ⚡ Thor Starter Active!
* With thor.enabled=false or property missing → prints Thor starter disabled.

🚀 Extensions
You can add more conditional annotations:
* @ConditionalOnClass(SomeLibrary.class) → only load if a class is on the classpath.
* @ConditionalOnMissingBean → only load if user hasn’t defined their own bean.
* Combine multiple conditions for flexible starters.

